hume-induction.md:3: argue/readings: S9 is read 2 ways: hume-induction.logic.r1 has "causal ∧ fromexp ∧ presupp → circular"; hume-induction.logic.r1.2 and hume-induction.logic.r1.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "all arguments concerning existence are founded on the relation of cause and effect", "our knowledge of the relation of cause and effect is derived entirely from experience" and "all our experimental conclusions proceed upon the supposition that the future will be conformable to the past" are true, which the second allows
hume-induction.md:3: argue/answered: S9: answered no, which keeps hume-induction.logic.r1, to: Suppose "all arguments concerning existence are founded on the relation of cause and effect", "our knowledge of the relation of cause and effect is derived entirely from experience" and "all our experimental conclusions proceed upon the supposition that the future will be conformable to the past" are true and "proving the supposition that the future will be conformable to the past by probable arguments is going in a circle, taking for granted the point in question" is false. Is S9, "To endeavour, therefore, the proof of this last supposition by probable arguments, or arguments regarding existence, must be evidently going in a circle, and taking that for granted, which is the very point in question.", true then?
hume-induction.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: hume-induction.logic.r1 has "circular → ¬probable"; hume-induction.logic.r1.2 and hume-induction.logic.r1.3 have "causal ∧ fromexp ∧ presupp → circular" and "circular → ¬probable"; they differ when only "there is a probable argument (concerning matter of fact and existence) that the future will be conformable to the past", "all arguments concerning existence are founded on the relation of cause and effect", "our knowledge of the relation of cause and effect is derived entirely from experience" and "all our experimental conclusions proceed upon the supposition that the future will be conformable to the past" are true, which the first allows [in 2 of 3 runs]
hume-induction.logic.r1: argue/readings: the main conclusion is read at 2 sentences: hume-induction.logic.r1 puts it at S6, "If we be, therefore, engaged by arguments to put trust in past experience, and make it the standard of our future judgement, these arguments must be probable only, or such as regard matter of fact and real existence, according to the division above mentioned."; hume-induction.logic.r1.2 and hume-induction.logic.r1.3 at S7, "But that there is no argument of this kind, must appear, if our explication of that species of reasoning be admitted as solid and satisfactory." [in 2 of 3 runs]
hume-induction.logic.r1: argue/answered: M: answered no, which keeps hume-induction.logic.r1.2 and hume-induction.logic.r1.3, to: Is the text's main conclusion S6, "If we be, therefore, engaged by arguments to put trust in past experience, and make it the standard of our future judgement, these arguments must be probable only, or such as regard matter of fact and real existence, according to the division above mentioned.", rather than S7, "But that there is no argument of this kind, must appear, if our explication of that species of reasoning be admitted as solid and satisfactory."? [in 1 of 3 runs]
hume-induction.md:1: argue/unfaithful: hume-induction.logic.r1 P1 (S1): the sentence has no word of "we are engaged by arguments to put trust in past experience and make it the standard of our future judgement": is it the sentence P1 came from?
hume-induction.md:1: argue/negation: hume-induction.logic.r1 P2 (S3): the sentence says "not", but P2 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "that the course of nature may change, and like objects be attended with contrary effects, can be clearly and distinctly conceived"
hume-induction.md:3: argue/unfaithful: hume-induction.logic.r1 P6 (S9): the sentence has no word of "our knowledge of the relation of cause and effect is derived entirely from experience": is it the sentence P6 came from?
hume-induction.md:3: argue/negation: hume-induction.logic.r1 C (S6): the sentence states "we are engaged by arguments to put trust in past experience and make it the standard of our future judgement", with no word of denial, but C denies it: it reads "it is not so that we are engaged by arguments to put trust in past experience and make it the standard of our future judgement" [in 1 of 3 runs]
